 <p>This public edition retains the landscape analysis and missional product design framework. The product case study and development roadmap have been omitted; evaluation requirements and limitations have been retained in a shorter, general form. The original submitted paper is unchanged.</p>
 <h2>Abstract</h2>
 <p>English-speaking Bible apps pioneered digital access to Scripture, reaching over a billion people globally and offering production quality and formats familiar from other media. Other language communities have often received a more limited experience. Meanwhile, many spiritual apps have inherited Silicon Valley's engagement mechanics without adequately asking whether those mechanics serve formation. Growing content catalogues offer choice and recommendations, but receptive content remains largely generic.</p>
 <p>This paper examines these problems through cognitive science, behavioral data, Christian formation tradition, and the European post-Christian context. It focuses on spiritually liminal audiences: Europeans who have walked away from institutions but have not stopped seeking. Its hypothesis is that AI could support more personally responsive spiritual formation and make richer resources available to less-resourced language communities. Whether it can do so without commodifying spiritual practice or displacing human community remains an open question.</p>
 <p>The analysis develops a <strong>missional product design framework</strong>: seven principles and six diagnostic questions connecting theology to product development. Multimodal formation, resistance to spiritual commodification, grace-based mechanics, and a communal horizon constrain what responsible personalization could mean. A concluding evaluation section distinguishes business metrics from provisional formation indicators and signs of harm, and identifies findings that would count against the paper's hypotheses. The framework specifies requirements for future design and evaluation; it does not demonstrate that a particular product produces spiritual growth.</p>
 <h2>How this site builds on it</h2>
 <p>Missional by Design grew out of this paper. The <a href={`${base}/framework/`}>principles and diagnostic questions</a> develop its framework for product teams. The <a href={`${base}/patterns/`}>patterns</a> apply it to concrete decisions, from <a href={`${base}/patterns/progress-and-streaks/`}>progress and streaks</a> to <a href={`${base}/patterns/notifications/`}>notifications</a>, and the <a href={`${base}/apps/`}>app library</a> documents what faith apps actually do. The paper's account of how faith apps inherited the attention economy's design philosophy is the starting point for the site's work on discerning common technology practices.</p>
 <aside aria-label="About this paper">
  <h2>About this paper</h2>
  <p>This is a research paper, not a peer-reviewed publication. Its framework remains a proposal rather than a validated account of formation outcomes. This adaptation does not comprehensively update the paper's empirical claims or source base. The Duomo discussion in §1.6 is a dated public-edition update based on sources checked on 1 November 2026. Book quotations and page references still need checking against licensed editions. Feedback and critique are welcome via the <a href={`${base}/about/`}>About page</a>.</p>
 </aside>
